featureBitsAbTest: check a featureBits build against the release binary
=======================================================================

Redmine #38774 (test bed), first used for #38773 (speedup) and #38780
(range check).

The test runs two featureBits binaries on the same real inputs with the same
options. For every case it compares the exit code, stdout, stderr and the md5
of each output file (-bed, -bin, -fa). Run it after any change to featureBits.c.

The current work directory is /hive/users/braney/fbAbTest38774/full:

  - 1,850 cases over hg38, hg19, mm39 and hs1: liftOver chains, PSL tables,
    BED files made from a sample of bigBeds, a few bigBeds read directly, and
    cases that use -or, -not, -enrichment, -minSize, -countBlocks, -bed, -fa.
  - 19 GB of inputs.
  - Reference results from the release binary, frozen as bin/featureBits.ref
    (checked against bin/featureBits.ref.md5).

The reference results are kept, so a new build only needs its own runs.


Checking a new build
--------------------

    T=~/kent/src/hg/featureBits/tests/featureBitsAbTest
    W=/hive/users/braney/fbAbTest38774/full
    $T run $W myChange ~/bin/x86_64/featureBits --cluster
    $T compare $W myChange

"myChange" is any name for the build. The binary is copied to
bin/featureBits.myChange, so a later rebuild does not change a run that is in
progress. Results go to runs/myChange/<caseId>/.

The new code takes about 2.6 CPU hours in all. Without --cluster every case
runs on hgwdev (-j sets how many at once).

compare prints counts of SAME, DIFF, EXPECTED and MISSING and lists each
DIFF. compare.myChange.tsv has one row per case with times and peak memory
for both binaries.

  - SAME: exit code, stdout, stderr and output files all match.
  - DIFF: something differs. The "differs" column says what. Compare
    runs/ref/<caseId>/ with runs/myChange/<caseId>/.
  - EXPECTED: differs, and the case is listed in expected.tsv with a reason.
  - MISSING: one side has no result yet.

Other options for run:

    --only REGEX   run only cases whose caseId matches
    --force        rerun cases that already have results
    --ram 8g       parasol -ram per job
    --rounds 6     cluster rounds before giving up (see below)


expected.tsv
------------

A case whose result is meant to change goes in expected.tsv as
"caseId<TAB>reason". It is then reported as EXPECTED, not DIFF. At present it
lists the 13 cases where a BED or bigBed item ends past its chromosome. The
release binary crashes on them; since #38780 featureBits stops with a
"Coordinate out of allowed range" error.

When a release binary with an intended change becomes the reference, rerun
the ref side (below) and empty expected.tsv.


Cluster notes
-------------

hgwdev is the parasol head node. The cluster nodes have no /gbdb and no
MySQL, so cluster cases use real paths under /hive, -chromSize and
-countGaps. The 12 cases that need the database (gap counts, -fa) are named
local.* and always run on hgwdev.

Each job runs through batch/<side>/runOne.sh, which writes stderr to
batch/<side>/logs/ because the nodes' own stderr files cannot be read from
hgwdev.

In November 2026 hgcompute-01 and hgcompute-13 failed every job with exit 1.
para marks a node sick after 25 failures in a row. run therefore loops: para
make, then para clearSickNodes, then resubmit the cases that have no exit
file, up to --rounds times.


Rebuilding the reference or the inputs
--------------------------------------

To make the release binary the new reference, rerun the ref side. It reads
each file once per chromosome, so it needs about 260 CPU hours on the cluster,
and the slowest cases take over 4 hours:

    $T run $W ref /cluster/bin/x86_64/featureBits --cluster --force

run refuses a ref binary that does not match bin/featureBits.ref.md5. Replace
bin/featureBits.ref and its md5 first.

To build a new work directory from scratch:

    $T setup NEWDIR

setup copies the release binary, dumps chrom sizes from chromInfo (hs1 uses
/hive/data/genomes/hs1/chrom.sizes), dumps the PSL tables, converts a sample
of bigBeds to BED, and writes cases.tsv. The bigBed sample comes from the
#38769 manifest, kept at /hive/users/braney/fbAbTest38774/manifest38769.tsv.
bigBeds over 10M items or 200 MB are skipped, so the test does not cover
larger BED files.
